  "query_preview": "..\n\nHeight rises are underway this afternoon as a departing shortwave \nflattens and gets absorbed downstream into the longwave trough/upper \nlow parked over Hudson Bay. Meanwhile, the next embedded speed max \ncarves through Northern Michigan this evening and overnight, \nreversing geopotential height trends. Weak CVA is evident in mid \nlevel progs which could provide sufficient ascent atop shallow \nboundary-layer saturation. This might spur a few light \nshowers/sprinkles into the early overnight hours, mainly over The \nThumb where moisture parameters are more favorable. Prior to 20Z a \nlone meso-gamma scale shower (or virga) was noted per KDTX \nreflectivity data.\n\nSurface high pressure centers over the Upper Midwest and Mid-\nMississippi Valley converge on Lower Michigan Friday with weakening \nnortherly flow in the wake of a secondary cold front. This helps \noffset high temperatures by a couple of degrees, near or slightly \nbelow 80F as 850 mb temperatures briefly dip into single digit \nterritory (Celsius). High pressure quickly exits across the eastern \nGreat Lakes Friday night with return flow resulting in gradient \nwinds flipping southwesterly. A shorter wavelength southern stream \nridge moves across The Plains on Saturday. Dry conditions continue \nanother day, while a warming trend also gets underway Saturday which \nshould last into the first half of next week. Warm advection and \nisentropic ascent-driven showers could develop as early as Sunday \nmorning with higher confidence in occurrence for areas north of I-\n96. The ridge axis amplifies and centers over the state on Monday. \nPrecipitation chances will then be governed by MCS activity until \nmid-week as a muggy and unstable air mass situates in the vicinity.",
